PROPOSED, NOT APPLIED — retarget the LDtk player-preview tileset to sprites_0_25x

    cd <repo root> && patch -p1 < dev/patches/ldtk-player-tileset-retarget-20260902.patch
    # or: git apply dev/patches/ldtk-player-tileset-retarget-20260902.patch
    # regenerate: scripts/propose_ldtk_tileset_retarget.py --out <this file>

⛔ THE FILES ARE IN `game/ambition_map_assets`, WHICH IS A SUBMODULE. Applying
this changes the submodule's working tree and needs a commit there plus a
pointer bump here. That is why it is a patch and not a commit.

WHAT IT DOES. Five `.ldtk` worlds declare `sprite_player_robot_v3` with
`relPath=../sprites/player_robot_v3_spritesheet.png` — 3072x2468, 7.6 MP —
which `bevy_ecs_ldtk` loads at boot because it loads every project tileset.
Measured (`scripts/measure_ldtk_tileset_usage.py`): NO level layer in any of the
five uses that tileset. Its only consumer is one entity definition per world,
`PlayerStart`, which crops the top-left tile for an editor preview. So the
runtime decodes 7.6 MP at every boot and never draws it.

⛔ IT IS NOT A relPath SWAP, and that is the point of the patch. `tileRect`,
`uiTileRect`, `tileGridSize`, `pxWid` and `pxHei` are all in TILESET PIXEL
coordinates. Change only the path and the 256x256 crop that framed one animation
frame spans a third of a 832-pixel image — the preview breaks while the JSON
still looks plausible. Every one of those fields is recomputed here from the
real PNG header, and each crop is preserved as the same FRACTION of the image.

ⓘ TWO PRE-EXISTING INCONSISTENCIES THIS ALSO FIXES, neither of which was the
goal. The four ambition_content worlds declare `pxHei: 2484` against a
2468-pixel file. And sanic_speedway declares `1681x1728, tileGridSize 224` for
that same file — a different and larger error. ⚠ The patch preserves sanic's
CURRENT framing as a fraction, which means it preserves whatever that stale
declaration was already showing; if sanic's preview looks wrong afterwards, it
looked wrong before.

⚠ The tiers are not exact fractions and the x and y factors differ (0_25x of
3072x2468 is 832x653, not 768x617), so the rescaled extents are 69x67 rather
than a round number. Rounding of a pixel or two in an editor preview is
immaterial; a clean constant does not exist.

EFFECT: the boot decode for this tileset goes from 7.6 MP to ~0.54 MP.
Untested against the LDtk editor itself — that needs Jon opening a world.

